solution:
[Overview]
This is a system of linear equations problem. The goal is to find the value of three circles. The key idea is to represent the triangle and circle values by variables and solve the two given equations.

[Plan]
P1. Assign variables to the triangle and circle values.
P2. Translate the two given picture equations into algebraic equations.
P3. Solve the system for the circle value.
P4. Compute the value of three circles.
P5. Verify the result by substitution.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let T be the value of one triangle and C be the value of one circle.
S2. [symbolic] Translate the first equation -> {{3T + 2C = 21}}
S3. [symbolic] Translate the second equation -> {{2T + 3C = 19}}
S4. [symbolic] Add the equations -> {{(3T + 2C) + (2T + 3C) = 21 + 19 => 5T + 5C = 40}}
S5. [symbolic] Divide by 5 -> {{5T + 5C = 40 => T + C = 8}}
S6. [symbolic] Subtract the second equation from the first -> {{(3T + 2C) - (2T + 3C) = 21 - 19 => T - C = 2}}
S7. [symbolic] Solve T + C = 8 and T - C = 2 -> {{T = 5, C = 3}}
S8. [calc] Compute the sum of three circles -> <<3*3=9>>

[Check]
V1. Substitute T = 5 and C = 3 into the first equation -> <<3*5 + 2*3 = 21>>
V2. Substitute T = 5 and C = 3 into the second equation -> <<2*5 + 3*3 = 19>>
V3. Confirm three circles equals 9 -> <<3*3 = 9>>

[Answer]
\boxed{9}

