[Overview]
This is an analytic geometry and locus problem. The goal is to find the slope of the line on which all intersection points lie. The key idea is to solve the two line equations for x and y in terms of t, then eliminate t to get a linear relation in x and y.

[Plan]
P1. Solve the system for x and y in terms of the parameter t.
P2. Eliminate t to obtain a single linear equation relating x and y.
P3. Rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form.
P4. Identify the slope and verify with sample parameter values.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let (x, y) be the intersection point corresponding to a real value of t.
S2. [symbolic] Subtract the second equation from the first -> {{(x+2y)-(x-y)=7t+3-(2t-2) => 3y=5t+5}}
S3. [symbolic] Solve for y -> {{3y=5t+5 => y=(5t+5)/3}}
S4. [symbolic] Use x-y=2t-2 and substitute for y -> {{x=y+2t-2 => x=(11t-1)/3}}
S5. [symbolic] Eliminate t by using t=(3y-5)/5 -> {{t=(3y-5)/5 => x=(11y-20)/5}}
S6. [symbolic] Rearrange into slope-intercept form -> {{5x=11y-20 => y=(5/11)x+20/11}}

[Check]
V1. At t=0, the intersection is (-1/3, 5/3), and (5/11)(-1/3)+20/11 = 5/3, so it lies on y=(5/11)x+20/11.
V2. At t=1, the intersection is (10/3, 10/3), and (5/11)(10/3)+20/11 = 10/3, so it also lies on the same line.
V3. The coefficient of x in slope-intercept form is the slope, so the slope is 5/11.

[Answer]
\boxed{\frac{5}{11}}

raw_question:
In the diagram below, $\|\overrightarrow{OA}\| = 1,$ $\|\overrightarrow{OB}\| = 1,$ and $\|\overrightarrow{OC}\| = \sqrt{2}.$  Also, $\tan \angle AOC = 7$ and $\angle BOC = 45^\circ.$

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B, C, O;

A = (1,0);
B = (-0.6,0.8);
C = (0.2,1.4);
O = (0,0);

draw(O--A,Arrow(6));
draw(O--B,Arrow(6));
draw(O--C,Arrow(6));

label("$A$", A, E);
label("$B$", B, NW);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$O$", O, S);
[/asy]

There exist constants $m$ and $n$ so that
\[\overrightarrow{OC} = m \overrightarrow{OA} + n \overrightarrow{OB}.\]Enter the ordered pair $(m,n).$

